Red Man Syndrome (Vancomycin Flushing Syndrome) combined with Vancomycin-Induced Immune Thrombocytopenia (VITT)
1. Patient DetailsPatient Initials / Identifier: O.T.Age: 51 yearsGender: FemaleWeight: 68 kg
2. Suspected Adverse ReactionDescription of Reaction: Severe, rapid-onset erythematous flushing ("Red Man Syndrome") accompanied by sudden, profound drug-induced immune thrombocytopenia (platelet count dropped precipitously from $240 \times 10^9/\text{L}$ to $8 \times 10^9/\text{L}$) with diffuse petechiae and mucosal bleeding.Date of Reaction Onset: Day 6 of intravenous therapy (flushing occurred within 15 minutes of rapid IV infusion).Severity: Severe / Life-Threatening (high risk of intracranial hemorrhage due to severe thrombocytopenia)
3. Suspected Medication(s)Primary Suspect: Vancomycin 1.5 g IV every 12 hours for MRSA bacteremia.Date Started: Day 1Date Stopped: Day 6Action Taken: Infusion halted immediately; Vancomycin permanently discontinued.
4. Concomitant MedicationsMedication 1: Normal Saline IV infusion.Medication 2: Piperacillin-Tazobactam (discontinued on Day 3 following blood culture sensitivities).
5. Outcome & Action TakenAction Taken: Vancomycin withdrawn; patient switched to Daptomycin. IV Diphenhydramine administered for flushing; IVIG and platelet transfusions provided for severe immune thrombocytopenia.De-challenge Outcome: Erythema and hypotension resolved within 3 hours. Platelet counts began recovering by Day 3 post-discontinuation and fully normalized ($210 \times 10^9/\text{L}$) by Day 8.Re-challenge: Strictly contraindicated.Patient Outcome: Fully recovered without permanent adverse effects.
